, making them essential for empirical research and policy analysis.\n\nThe general form of a single-equation regression model can be expressed mathematically as:\n\n\\[ Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 X_1 + \\beta_2 X_2 + \\ldots + \\beta_k X_k + \\epsilon \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( Y \\) is the dependent variable,\n- \\( \\beta_0 \\) is the intercept,\n- \\( \\beta_1, \\beta_2, \\ldots, \\beta_k \\) are the coefficients of the independent variables \\( X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_k \\),\n- \\( \\epsilon \\) is the error term, capturing the effects of omitted variables, measurement errors, and inherent randomness.\n\nThe primary purpose of single-equation regression models is to estimate the parameters (\\( \\beta \\) coefficients) that describe how changes in the independent variables affect the dependent variable. This estimation is typically performed using the Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) method, which minimizes the sum of the squared differences between the observed and predicted values of the dependent variable.\n\n**Assumptions of Single-Equation Regression Models**\n\nFor the results obtained from single-equation regression models to be valid, several key assumptions must be satisfied:\n\n1. **Linearity**: The relationship between the dependent and independent variables is linear.\n2. **Independence**: The observations are independent of each other.\n3. **Homoscedasticity**: The variance of the error term (\\( \\epsilon \\)) is constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. **Normality**: The error term is normally distributed, particularly important for hypothesis testing.\n5. **No perfect multicollinearity**: The independent variables are not perfectly correlated with each other.\n\nThese assumptions are crucial because violations can lead to biased, inconsistent, or inefficient estimates. For instance, if the assumption of homoscedasticity is violated (i.e., heteroscedasticity is present), the standard errors of the estimates may be biased, leading to incorrect 